.TH READ_OPTION 3ggl "1 August 1997" "GGL/IGP ETHZ" "GGL Common Lib"
.SH NAME
read_option, read_soption \- read option entry following a specific character
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <common/inout.h>

.RB "char * " read_option
.RI "(FILE *" fpin ", const int " idchar ");"

.RB "char * " read_soption
.RI "(FILE *" fpin ", const int " idchar ");"
.SH DESCRIPTION
These functions read bytes from a stream specified by
.I fpin
until it finds a character identical to
.I idchar
or until it reaches end of file.

When using
.B read_option()
The remainder of the current line, starting with
the character following the
.I idchar
up to and including the newline character is stored
into a newly allocated string.

With
.BR read_soption() ,
only one string is returned as with the `%s' template
when using sscanf(3).
All preceeding white space will be overread, and
the string will be read up to a following white space
character. The returned string will not contain any of the
white space characters. Any subsequent strings or characters
following until the end of line will be dropped. 
.SH "RETURN VALUES"
.BR read_option() " and " read_soption()
return a pointer to this newly allocated string containing
the option information, unless an error occurred while
reading from the file. In case of an error or upon reaching
the end of file,
.BR read_option() " and " read_soption()
return a
.B NULL
pointer.

.B read_soption()
will also return a null pointer, when no string could
be found following the
.IR idchar .
This could be the case when there are only white space
characters as returned by isspace(3) following the
.IR idchar .

The function
.B read_soption()
was primarily introduced to quickly read filenames
as option contents from an input file. The return
value may thus easily be checked for the existence of such
an item.
.SH ERRORS
The function will call standard library functions
that may set
.IR errno .
If any called function produces an error, or if
the end of file is reached, the return value will be
set to
.BR NULL .
.SH CAVEATS
You have to compile your program using
.I -lcommon
as a compiler flag to properly link
your function to the library.
